Because Homo sapiens was not the first human lineage to leave Africa.

That is the piece that makes the whole picture click.

By roughly 1.8 million years ago—and possibly earlier—early Homo had already spread into Eurasia. Dmanisi in Georgia has Homo erectus–grade humans around 1.77–1.85 million years ago, and stone tools in China may go back about 2.1 million years. By ~1.7–1.6 million years ago, humans were living from northern China all the way to Java. 

So there were effectively older Out-of-Africa waves:

\text{Africa}
\rightarrow \text{early Homo dispersals}
\rightarrow \text{Eurasian populations}
\rightarrow \text{hundreds of thousands of years of evolution}

while other Homo populations remained in Africa.

Over enormous spans of time, those Eurasian populations became differentiated.

A simplified genealogy is something like:

earlier African Homo
        |
        | ~2 million years ago
        v
   Homo erectus / related populations
      /                        \
 Africa                         Eurasia
   |                               |
later African lineages       long local evolution
   |                          /             \
Homo sapiens            Neanderthals     Denisovans

The exact species labels in the middle—erectus, heidelbergensis, etc.—are debated, so don’t take that cartoon as a settled taxonomy. But the population-history principle is solid.

They had an enormous head start

When sapiens made the expansion that eventually produced most living non-Africans around 50–70 thousand years ago, they weren’t arriving in an empty continent.

Neanderthals had already been living across Europe and western Asia for hundreds of thousands of years. Genetic and fossil evidence puts the sapiens/Neanderthal divergence somewhere on the order of several hundred thousand years ago; Neanderthals likely evolved largely outside Africa. 

And Denisovans occupied large portions of eastern Eurasia. We now have evidence for Denisovans on the Tibetan Plateau at least 160,000 years ago, at ~3,280 meters altitude—an extremely challenging environment. 

So these weren’t tropical Africans somehow barely hanging on through an Ice Age.

They had undergone local biological and cultural adaptation for hundreds of millennia.

Neanderthals were genuinely cold-adapted humans

Their anatomy reflects it.

They tended to have:

* shorter distal limbs,
* broad, stocky bodies,
* very robust skeletons,
* large nasal regions,
* high muscularity.

A compact body loses less heat because it has less surface area relative to volume. That’s classic thermoregulation, and the Neanderthal skeleton fits it very well. 

But biology was only part of it.

They also:

* controlled fire,
* made sophisticated prepared-core stone tools,
* hunted large animals,
* processed hides,
* wore clothing,
* used shelters,
* exploited both animals and plants.

So their Eurasian survival strategy was already biocultural—genes + learned technology + social organization. 

And Eurasia wasn’t uniformly frozen

This is another easy misconception.

“Outside Africa” includes an absurd ecological range:

Georgia → Levant → Siberia → China → India → Indonesia.

Early Homo occupied temperate grassland, forests, lakeshores, dry environments, tropical woodland and coastal environments. At roughly 1.66 million years ago humans were already distributed from around 40° N in China to 7° S in Java. 

So the first dispersers did not necessarily have to leap directly from equatorial Africa into an Ice Age French winter.

Populations could expand along ecological corridors:

Africa \rightarrow Levant/Arabia \rightarrow Caucasus/South Asia
\rightarrow East Asia

and then gradually adapt as ranges shifted.

Climate itself repeatedly opened and closed those corridors.

Homo erectus is the really important character

If you zoom way out, Homo erectus is arguably the first great human colonizer.

It had:

* essentially human-like long-distance terrestrial locomotion,
* a larger brain than earlier hominins,
* increasingly sophisticated stone technology,
* probably increasingly important meat acquisition,
* eventually evidence associated with hearth/fire use,
* extraordinary ecological flexibility.

Smithsonian describes H. erectus as one of the widest dispersals in early human evolution. 

And it persisted astonishingly long.

Some Asian Homo erectus populations survived until roughly 100–150 thousand years ago, depending on which fossils/dates you accept.

So imagine what that means:

When early Homo sapiens already existed in Africa, populations descended from much earlier human dispersals were still living in Asia.

Humanity was not sequential:

erectus → Neanderthal → sapiens.

It was concurrent.

At various times Earth contained multiple forms of human simultaneously.

Which makes the meeting much more interesting

Around 60–50 thousand years ago, expanding sapiens populations entered territories whose inhabitants had evolutionary histories extending back hundreds of thousands of years.

Then:

AFRICAN SAPIENS
       |
       ↓
   Eurasia
       |
       +──── meet Neanderthals
       |          ↓
       |       interbreed
       |
       +──── move east
                  ↓
              Denisovans
                  ↓
               interbreed

And those meetings were fertile enough that their DNA remains in us.

That’s why most living people with substantial ancestry outside Africa carry Neanderthal-derived DNA, and some Asian and Oceanian populations carry additional Denisovan ancestry. 

One of the coolest examples is EPAS1, important in Tibetan high-altitude physiology. Modern Tibetans appear to have inherited the advantageous haplotype from a Denisovan-related population. 

So in at least one case the story is approximately:

Denisovans survive the Tibetan Plateau → sapiens arrives → admixture occurs → sapiens inherits an adaptation evolved in an archaic Eurasian population → descendants use it today.

That’s evolution doing a technology transfer through genomes.

And this changes how I picture human evolution

Don’t picture a species tree with one trunk relentlessly becoming us.

Picture successive diasporas.

D_1, D_2, D_3,\ldots,D_n

Africa repeatedly generates or contains populations that expand outward.

Earlier diaspora populations differentiate geographically.

Later diasporas encounter them.

Some older populations die out.

Some merge.

Some genetic material travels forward.

Some even appears to move back into Africa later.

So instead of:

Africa → modern humans → world

I’d draw:

                 EURASIA
        ╭──── early dispersal ────╮
        │                         │
AFRICA ─┼─────────────────────────┤
        │                         │
        ├── later dispersal ──────┤
        │        ↕ admixture      │
        ├── sapiens dispersal ────┤
        │        ↕ admixture      │
        ╰──── back migrations ────╯

That’s why I called it a graph rather than a tree.

And the deepest answer to your question is therefore almost paradoxical:

The Eurasians were themselves descendants of Africans who had left vastly earlier.

By the time our particular sapiens ancestors encountered them, though, 500,000+ years of separation in some lineages was enough that meeting them really was something like encountering another kind of human.

That makes the human past considerably stranger—and more populated—than the old textbook version suggests.